WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.220
President Kennedy begins a tour of four space installations at Huntsville, Alabama, where

00:08.220 --> 00:12.740
he is greeted by Dr. Verna von Braun, space pioneer and director of this research and

00:12.740 --> 00:17.840
development center. The missiles on display are dwarfed by the mighty Saturn rocket. This

00:17.840 --> 00:22.560
booster is the first stage of the rocket that will put the three-man Apollo capsule into

00:22.560 --> 00:28.540
a two-week orbit of the Earth in 1964 as a preliminary to a lunar shoot. The men who

00:28.540 --> 00:38.720
make it get very personal greetings. From Alabama to Cape Canaveral, next stop on the tour.

00:38.720 --> 00:43.720
At this U.S. space capital, the president has a long chat with Walter M. Schirra, center,

00:43.720 --> 00:48.040
our next man in space, who is scheduled for a six-orbit flight. The commander-in-chief

00:48.040 --> 00:54.620
learns from an expert. Next stop, Houston, where he is cheered by thousands of Texans

00:54.620 --> 00:59.040
on his way to a stadium for his key speech of the trip. At Rice University, there's a

00:59.040 --> 01:04.080
crowd of 40,000 present, and Mr. Kennedy calls for backing for the U.S. space effort. He

01:04.080 --> 01:14.040
says that we cannot afford to lag if we want to be in the forefront of nations. Then to

01:14.040 --> 01:19.320
the manned spacecraft center, where the $20 billion Apollo project is in high gear. Here,

01:19.320 --> 01:24.800
the president sees mock-ups like the moon bug that is being prepared for a lunar landing,

01:24.800 --> 01:30.040
and the Gemini, a larger Mercury capsule for two men and other space vehicles. The president

01:30.040 --> 01:34.960
receives a briefing on highly classified matter and seems pleased with the progress report

01:34.960 --> 01:40.240
when he inspects the apparatus that the U.S. is making ready for further conquest of space.

01:40.240 --> 01:49.600
It was in Houston that Mr. Kennedy emphasized the need for peaceful uses of space. Fourth

01:49.600 --> 01:54.200
and final stop is at St. Louis for an inspection of the McDonnell plant, where the Phantom

01:54.200 --> 02:00.680
II fighter bomber is built, as well as Mercury and Gemini capsules. The F4-H is a 1,600-mile-an-hour

02:00.680 --> 02:09.120
streak of lightning. The Gemini, which was developed here, will allow two men to orbit

02:09.120 --> 02:13.280
the Earth for two weeks and will be used for experiments in outer space rendezvous and

02:13.280 --> 02:18.400
docking missions. This winds up the president's firsthand look at our space program and our

02:18.400 --> 02:33.560
progress in the race for space. Leather is falling into step in the fall fashion parade.

02:33.560 --> 02:38.240
Finished in an explosion of rainbow colors, leather has been styled into smocks and sheathed

02:38.240 --> 02:42.960
top skirts like this eye-stopping pair, just the thing for a day's outing at Sterling Forest

02:42.960 --> 02:48.960
Gardens in New York. On the left, a pile-line coat, and on the right, a natural pigskin,

02:48.960 --> 02:55.360
leather to get a man in a lather. Designers have let their imaginations run riot. They've

02:55.360 --> 03:03.320
even captured a leopard to line this hooded suede. Another offbeat treatment of suede,

03:03.320 --> 03:08.080
a shift with a cardigan jacket, and the suede hat offers Milady a chance to add another

03:08.080 --> 03:15.160
feather to her cap. To enhance the classic tweed, this three-piece suit is trimmed with

03:15.160 --> 03:24.360
suede and hidden beneath the sweeping lines of the coat is a leather blouse. And here,

03:24.360 --> 03:34.280
the shift is treated with a saucy bolero jacket all topped with a kidskin turban. The chest

03:34.280 --> 03:38.760
revealed in suede with a matching tote bag. These are the fashions aimed at women who

03:38.760 --> 04:04.920
want something different, and maybe one woman who doesn't.

